from pathlib import Path
from shutil import copyfile
from docx import Document
P=Path(__file__).resolve().parent; path=P.parent/'redshift_paper/temporal_redshift_paper.docx'
doc=Document(path)
assert not any(x.text.startswith('Appendix E.') for x in doc.paragraphs)
copyfile(path,P/'paper_before.docx');original=[x._p.xml for x in doc.paragraphs]
def p(t,s='Normal'):doc.add_paragraph(t,s)
def h(t):p(t,'Heading 2')
def e(t):p(t,'Equation')
doc.add_page_break()
p('Appendix E. Shared interactions and a fixed higher-redshift brightness test','Heading 1')
p('This extension tests the fixed-atom candidate at two levels: a common matter–light–gravity action, and a higher-redshift brightness prediction with the original coefficient unchanged. The action is a proposed low-energy completion, not an established theory. Its atomic and gravitational difficulties are retained explicitly. The brightness calculation uses real standardized observations and their released covariance, with no new fit of the redshift law.')
h('E.1 A concrete common action')
p('In units c₀=ℏ=1 and signature (−,+,+,+), use a matter metric g, a positive propagation field n, and a dynamical vector u with uμuμ=−1. Define the optical metric ḡμν=gμν+(1−n⁻²)uμuν. A candidate action is the sum:')
e('S = S_EH[g] + Sₙ[g,n] + Sᵤ[g,u] + S_m[g,A,Ψ] + S_EM[ḡ,A,n].')
p('The Einstein term is M_Pl²ℛ/2. The scalar density is −K(∂n)²/2−V(n). A concrete preferred-frame vector density is −βUμνUμν/4+λ(uμuμ+1), with U=du and K,β>0. Matter has fixed masses and charges and couples to the same potential A as the electromagnetic action. The latter is −¼∫√(−ḡ) Z(n) ḡμαḡνβFμνFαβ d⁴x, where F=dA and Z>0. Gauge symmetry enforces charge conservation, and total stress-energy is covariantly conserved on the complete equations of motion. Related disformal frameworks are discussed in [20].')
p('The vector replaces an externally imposed preferred direction with a dynamical field; its longitudinal stability, constraints and strong-coupling behavior are not established. Positive scalar and Maxwell coefficients alone do not certify full stability. Nuclear structure and quantum corrections also remain outside this low-energy specification.')
p('In the local matter rest frame, ḡ=diag(−n⁻²,1,1,1). The electromagnetic density and constitutive response become:')
e('ℒ_EM = (Zn/2)E² − [Z/(2n)]B²;     εᵣ=Zn,     μᵣ=n/Z,     cγ=c₀/n.')
p('With fixed masses and charges, leading hydrogen energies scale as (Zn)⁻² and atomic lengths as Zn. Preserving both selects Z=1/n, yielding εᵣ=1 and μᵣ=n². Electrostatic binding is then unchanged, but magnetic interactions are not. Since εᵣμᵣ=n², the single function Z cannot keep both constitutive responses constant while n evolves. This restricted result exposes the unfinished atomic-protection assumption in Appendix D; it is not a universal impossibility theorem.')

doc.add_page_break()
h('E.2 Atomic, gravitational and propagation consistency')
p('For fixed magnetic moments and leading wavefunctions, hyperfine frequencies scale approximately as n². A leading optical/hyperfine ratio therefore drifts at −2ṅ/n, or −1.5463×10⁻¹⁰ yr⁻¹ for the fixed γ today. Lange et al. directly report a Yb⁺ E3/Cs ratio drift of −3.1(3.4)×10⁻¹⁷ yr⁻¹ [10]. This comparison uses the measured ratio rather than importing the paper’s standard fine-structure-constant interpretation. The scale mismatch is severe, but an exact exclusion requires species-specific relativistic, magnetic and nuclear calculations. The simplest branch already fails its intended exact atomic invariance; added couplings would need to protect multiple observables.')
p('The shared action does recover the earlier propagation-energy source. With Z=1/n its scalar source is B²/n³; for a traveling wave in the homogeneous rest frame this equals uγ/n. Thus the reduced equation K n̈+V′=uγ/n follows at that order. Radiation energy transferred to n remains part of the gravitational source.')
p('Ordinary Einstein gravity adds an independent obstruction. A flat homogeneous static solution needs both ρ_total=0 and ρ_total+p_total=0. At today’s n=1, the two metrics coincide. In the homogeneous vector rest branch U=0 and λ=0 at that epoch, ordinary baryons, radiation and the rolling canonical scalar give:')
e('ρ_total + p_total = ρ_b + (4/3)ρ_γ + Kγ² > 0.')
p('A constant potential or cosmological constant does not cancel this sum. The assumed flat static background is therefore not a solution of this simplest complete action. Positive spatial curvature changes the balance and leaves a spherical static candidate open, but its evolution and perturbation stability must be solved [24]. Curvature would also change the geometric brightness law and must be labeled as a new extension.')
p('Tensor waves in the minimal Einstein sector travel on g at c₀; photons travel on ḡ at c₀/n. Present cone equality does not imply equal integrated travel times. For affine n, simultaneous emission at distance R and photon reception today give:')
e('Δt_GW−γ = R/c₀ − [1−exp(−γR/c₀)]/γ.')
p('At an illustrative 40 Mpc the gravitational wave arrives about 655,762 years later. The associated GW170817/gamma-ray event has a published gamma-ray-minus-GW delay of 1.74±0.05 seconds [14]. Source emission delays and modified-theory distance inference remain relevant, but this enormous benchmark discrepancy identifies a major failure of the minimal cone assignment. A gravitational sector sharing the optical cone is retained as a possible extension; it would change the field equations as well.')

doc.add_page_break()
h('E.3 Frozen higher-redshift brightness prediction')
p('The original κ=0.000077315 per million light-years, S=exp(κR), and D_L=RS are held fixed. For Euclidean static geometry, fixed source luminosity and photon conservation, the energy and arrival-rate factors each reduce flux by S, giving D_L=(1+z)ln(1+z)/κ. This distance function also appears in coasting expansion models [23], so agreement would not uniquely identify this mechanism.')
p('The official Pantheon+SH0ES release [21,22] is pinned to commit c447f0fea703fcd0fff57de5000947b5ca81286b. Before inspecting brightness outcomes, the analysis specified zHD≥0.1 for noncalibrators and fixed all formula parameters. The primary observer convention uses (1+zHEL) outside the logarithm and zHD inside it. Its transfer to this theory is conditional and is checked separately. The protocol is internally frozen, not an external preregistration.')
p('Only 77 Cepheid-host light-curve rows, representing 43 distinct supernovae, determine absolute magnitude. Generalized least squares on m_b_corr−CEPH_DIST gives M=−19.248885±0.030106, with χ²=57.135 for 76 degrees of freedom. No higher-redshift brightness is used for that calibration. The evaluation contains 960 distinct supernovae at zHD=0.10165–2.26137. Calibrator repeated measurements retain their released covariance.')
p('Let w=Ccc⁻¹1/(1ᵀCcc⁻¹1), σM²=wᵀCccw and q=Chcw. The higher-redshift residual covariance is V=Chh+σM²11ᵀ−q1ᵀ−1qᵀ, preserving shared calibrator systematics. The full STAT+SYS matrix is used. Its small released rounding asymmetry, at most 3×10⁻⁸ mag², is removed by symmetrization. Positive residuals below mean observed standardized supernovae are fainter than predicted.')
p('For z=0.10–0.30, 466 supernovae give +0.1383±0.0314 mag, equivalent to a 6.6% distance excess. For z=0.30–0.60, 365 give +0.2004±0.0323 mag, or 9.7%. For z=0.60–1.00, 104 give +0.2182±0.0380 mag, or 10.6%. For z=1.00–2.26, 25 give +0.3631±0.0708 mag, or 18.2%. These generalized-least-squares bin means are correlated; their full covariance is saved.')
p('Accounting for those correlations, the second minus first bin is +0.06208±0.01271 mag and the highest minus first is +0.22487±0.06460 mag. A constant residual across the four bins gives χ²=36.743 for three degrees of freedom. This identifies a redshift-dependent issue rather than counting shared calibration errors as independent evidence.')

doc.add_page_break()
h('E.4 Interpretation, limits and retained extensions')
p('The fixed temporal law gives χ²=898.475 for 960 residuals and unweighted RMSE=0.2421 mag. A fixed flat-ΛCDM reference with Ωm=0.3 and the same scale H₀=c₀κ=75.5980 km s⁻¹ Mpc⁻¹ gives χ²=838.483 and RMSE=0.1555 mag. Neither reference parameter is fitted here. A labeled shape-only diagnostic subtracting one constant offset gives χ²=871.552 and 836.512, respectively, for 959 degrees of freedom. Those fitted diagnostic offsets are not applied to the primary predictions or to κ.')
p('The temporal model’s total χ² is below the number of residuals, so it is not a catastrophic global goodness-of-fit rejection under this covariance. The meaningful issues are the coherent bin trend and the poorer relative fit. Using zHD for both redshift factors changes any predicted magnitude by at most 0.00253 mag and gives χ²=897.936. A conditional-Gaussian calibrator sensitivity treatment gives 907.720. Neither removes the discrepancy. No opacity, luminosity evolution, age correction or radiation-backreaction parameter is fitted to improve these results.')
p('The release contains standardized magnitudes, not raw photons. SALT2, color/stretch and host corrections, selection simulations, intrinsic scatter and parts of the velocity uncertainty carry published assumptions. The Cepheid ladder adds calibration assumptions. The requested fixed coefficient has no fitted high-redshift adjustment or propagated uncertainty band in this primary test. Some objects may overlap earlier duration studies, so this is a new observable evaluation rather than an entirely independent sky sample. A forward light-curve and selection analysis is needed to assess processing dependence.')
p('The two tracks therefore remain distinct. The common action has not solved exact atomic protection, nonexpanding gravitational dynamics or tensor-wave propagation. The fixed-source phenomenological law has a substantive higher-redshift brightness trend. Neither result is repaired by silently changing a coupling, curvature, source luminosity or distance scale. All branches and discrepancies are retained. The next theoretical milestone is a shared coupling that protects optical/hyperfine ratios and aligns tensor propagation while admitting a stable nonexpanding solution. The next observational milestone is an independent or forward-modeled brightness test with calibration fixed in advance.')
h('Additional references and reproducibility for Appendix E')
p('[21] Brout, D., et al. The Pantheon+ Analysis: Cosmological Constraints (2022). https://arxiv.org/abs/2202.04077','Reference')
p('[22] PantheonPlusSH0ES. DataRelease: distances, covariance and README, pinned commit stated in E.3. https://github.com/PantheonPlusSH0ES/DataRelease/tree/main/Pantheon%2B_Data/4_DISTANCES_AND_COVAR','Reference')
p('[23] Rh = ct universe and quintessence. MNRAS 457, 212 (2016), luminosity-distance relation. https://academic.oup.com/mnras/article/457/1/212/989371','Reference')
p('[24] Barrow, J. D., et al. On the Stability of the Einstein Static Universe (2003). https://arxiv.org/abs/gr-qc/0302094','Reference')
p('Companion: shared_interaction_test.zip includes the full derivation report, frozen protocol, official data and covariance with hashes, brightness.py, interaction.py, results and all 960 predictions. No raw supernova light-curve or spacecraft residual fit is claimed.','Reference')
assert [x._p.xml for x in doc.paragraphs[:len(original)]]==original
doc.save(path)
print('Original paragraphs preserved:',len(original),'Total:',len(doc.paragraphs))
